Subject: net: use WASIX sockets on wasip1 with the wasix build tag
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

Without socket calls, wasip1 answers Listen and Dial from an in-process
fake network. With the wasix build tag, socket opens a real socket
through the syscall package and binds and listens it or connects it; the
descriptor is then served by the same non-blocking reads, writes and
poll_oneoff waits as an inherited socket. As on the Unix ports, a stream
connect waits for the socket to become writable, within the context's
deadline, and confirms it with SO_ERROR and the peer's address; a
datagram socket connects before the poller registers it; and a Control
function runs before the bind or connect. The datagram methods go to the
descriptor, the address-typed ones through the generic calls, and File
fails, since WASI preview 1 cannot duplicate a descriptor. The fake
network's socket moves to a file of its own.

On every wasip1 build, an accepted connection carries the peer's
address, where the runtime reports one, and the listener's as its local
address. Keep-alive, no-delay and the buffer sizes go to the runtime,
and a stream listener reuses its address, so a restarted server binds at
once. The runtime has no keep-alive idle time, interval or count, so
setting those succeeds and keeps its defaults.

